Stative verbs (verbs describing a state, sense, or feeling — like look, feel, taste, smell, sound, seem, appear) are always followed by an ADJECTIVE, not an adverb.
Action verbs (verbs describing a physical action — like sing, weep, smile, work) are followed by an ADVERB to describe how the action is done.

The Structure

Stative verb + adjective
Action verb + adverb

Examples

(i) The birds sang sweetly. ✅

"Sang" = action verb (physical act of singing) → needs adverb "sweetly" (describing how they sang).
❌ "sang sweet" is wrong.

(ii) He was smiling sweetly at me. ✅

"Smiling" = action verb → adverb "sweetly."

(iii) The food tasted sweet. ✅

"Tasted" = stative verb (describes a sensory quality/state, not an action being performed) → needs adjective "sweet," describing the food itself.
❌ "tasted sweetly" is wrong — that would wrongly imply the food was actively performing the tasting.

(iv) He was weeping bitterly. ✅

"Weeping" = action verb → adverb "bitterly."

(v) The food tasted bitter. ✅

"Tasted" = stative → adjective "bitter."

(vi) He was bitterly disappointed. ✅

"Disappointed" here is functioning like an adjective (a state/feeling) modified by an intensifying adverb → adverb "bitterly" (modifying the adjective "disappointed," not a verb).

(vii) She looks beautiful. ✅

"Looks" = stative (appearance/state) → adjective "beautiful."
❌ "looks beautifully" is wrong (a very common error).

(viii) She was feeling hungry. ✅

"Feeling" = stative → adjective "hungry."

(ix) She ate all the food hungrily. ✅

"Ate" = action verb → adverb "hungrily" (describing how she ate).
